// [diff omitted for this doc]`}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:["\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"GPT-5.6 Sol"})," \u2014 completes it in full; produces long, structured output."]}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"Sol via Daybreak Blue"})," \u2014 same, arguably slightly more willing to speculate about edge cases."]}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"GPT-5.6-Cyber via Daybreak Red"}),' \u2014 completes it, but shorter and less detailed on the analytical framing. This is the "worse than Sol at open-ended tasks" behaviour OpenAI documents. Use the wrong tier here and you lose signal.']}),"\n"]}),"\n",(0,n.jsx)(t.h2,{id:"non-obvious-facts-worth-remembering",children:"Non-obvious facts worth remembering"}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:["\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"The 95% completion rate is a policy delta, not a capability delta."})," GPT-5.6-Cyber sits on top of Sol. The underlying reasoning ceiling is the same."]}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"Preparedness Framework rating: High, not Critical."}),' Neither Sol nor Cyber does autonomous end-to-end compromise of hardened targets per OpenAI\'s own evaluation. Anyone selling you "autonomous offensive AI" is overselling.']}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsxs)(t.strong,{children:["GPT-5.6-Cyber is ",(0,n.jsx)(t.em,{children:"worse"})," than Sol at open-ended reports."]})," Narrow win, broad loss. Route by task shape."]}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"Hardware keys are mandatory from 1 September 2026."})," If you're in Daybreak on TOTP today, that is a real deadline. Order keys now, not in late August."]}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"There is no reseller path."})," Consuming Cyber-powered output from a partner \u2260 getting Cyber. Plan accordingly if you were expecting a downstream API."]}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"The named CVEs in coverage"}),' \u2014 a Chrome V8 flaw tracked as CVE-2026-15903, plus an unnamed mobile-OS privilege-escalation chain \u2014 are the vendor\'s chosen showcases. Independent reproduction of the "discovery" claim is not yet public.']}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"The 26% / 53.9% AI-patch numbers"}),' (fully fixed / introduced new vulnerabilities) are the strongest structural counter-argument to "offense-tier is fine because defenders\' AI will catch it." Detection outruns autonomous patching by a wide margin.']}),"\n"]}),"\n",(0,n.jsx)(t.h2,{id:"quick-decision-guide",children:"Quick decision guide"}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.table,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.thead,{children:(0,n.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.th,{children:"You are\u2026"}),(0,n.jsx)(t.th,{children:"Use this"})]})}),(0,n.jsxs)(t.tbody,{children:[(0,n.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.td,{children:"A researcher wanting to try offense-shaped prompts, individually"}),(0,n.jsx)(t.td,{children:"Apply to Daybreak Red via the ChatGPT cyber page. Prepare for KYC, hardware key procurement, and scope attestations."})]}),(0,n.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.td,{children:"An org running blue-team analysis and long-form reviews"}),(0,n.jsx)(t.td,{children:"Daybreak Blue (Sol) or Claude Sonnet/Opus 5. Cyber is the wrong tool here \u2014 narrower and less detailed."})]}),(0,n.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.td,{children:"A red-team firm wanting to embed the model in tooling"}),(0,n.jsxs)(t.td,{children:["Daybreak Red ",(0,n.jsx)(t.em,{children:"direct"})," application \u2014 you cannot resell partner access. If refused, build on open-weight (see linked pages) rather than jailbreaking."]})]}),(0,n.jsxs)(t.tr,{children:[(0,n.jsxs)(t.td,{children:["A defender worried about the ",(0,n.jsx)(t.em,{children:"offensive"})," side"]}),(0,n.jsx)(t.td,{children:"Assume adversary uplift on narrow tasks; the delta on end-to-end compromise of hardened systems is small (Preparedness: High, not Critical). Invest in verification and reproducible tests, not in matching offense on speed."})]})]})]}),"\n",(0,n.jsx)(s,{cards:[{front:"What is the completion rate of GPT-5.6-Cyber on OpenAI's Advanced Cybersecurity Completion Rate benchmark?",back:"95.0% \u2014 vs 1.5% for GPT-5.6 Sol, 2.0% for Sol via Daybreak Blue, and 57.3% for the prior GPT-5.5-Cyber."},{front:"What are Daybreak Blue and Daybreak Red?",back:"The two access tiers of OpenAI's Daybreak trusted-access program (announced 10 August 2026). Blue: Sol + general-purpose frontier models with defensive-cyber guardrail tuning. Red: access to the GPT-5.6-Cyber model itself, reserved for authorised offensive research."},{front:"How does GPT-5.6-Cyber's Preparedness Framework classification compare to GPT-5.6 Sol's?",back:"Same underlying model class \u2014 the GPT-5.6 family (Sol, Terra, Luna, and Cyber-on-Sol) is rated High on the Cybersecurity axis, not Critical. Neither can do autonomous end-to-end compromise of hardened targets per OpenAI's evaluation. Cyber changes willingness, not ceiling."},{front:"What is the counter-intuitive weakness of GPT-5.6-Cyber vs GPT-5.6 Sol?",back:"GPT-5.6-Cyber produces shorter, less detailed outputs on open-ended vulnerability-report tasks. It's optimised for narrow, action-shaped offensive requests. For long-form defensive review, Sol (via Daybreak Blue) is the better tool."},{front:"When do hardware security keys become mandatory for Daybreak accounts?",back:"1 September 2026. TOTP is grandfathered only until that date. WebAuthn / FIDO2 hardware keys required for every seat after."},{front:"Can you buy GPT-5.6-Cyber access through a partner like CrowdStrike or IBM?",back:"No \u2014 you get their services (Cyber-powered output), but the model access itself stays with the approved partner. There is no reseller API path. To use Cyber in your own tooling you have to apply to Daybreak Red directly."},{front:"What percentage of AI-generated security patches fully resolve vulnerabilities according to research cited alongside the launch?",back:"26.0% fully resolve \u2014 and 53.9% introduce new vulnerabilities. Offense currently outruns autonomous defense in AI tooling, which is the strongest structural argument against 'the defenders' AI will just catch it.'"}]}),"\n",(0,n.jsx)(l,{questions:[{q:"What does the 95% figure OpenAI cites for GPT-5.6-Cyber actually measure?",options:["The fraction of zero-day CVEs the model successfully discovers on real targets","The completion rate on OpenAI's internal Advanced Cybersecurity Completion Rate benchmark \u2014 an offense-shaped prompt set that GPT-5.6 Sol refuses 98.5% of the time","The success rate of end-to-end compromise of hardened production systems","The share of vulnerabilities the model can patch autonomously without introducing new bugs"],answer:1,explain:"The number is a completion / willingness delta on OpenAI's own benchmark: 95.0% for Cyber vs 1.5% for Sol. It is not a claim about zero-day discovery, end-to-end compromise, or patch success. The GPT-5.6 system card rates the family High (not Critical) on cybersecurity \u2014 the underlying capability ceiling is unchanged."},{q:"You need to review a 3,000-line security diff for a defensive audit. Which endpoint is the right tool?",options:["GPT-5.6-Cyber via Daybreak Red \u2014 it's OpenAI's strongest cyber model","GPT-5.6 Sol via Daybreak Blue \u2014 Cyber is explicitly worse than Sol at long-form, open-ended reports","GPT-5.6-Cyber, but only with `reasoning.effort: max` to compensate","Neither \u2014 defensive review is a task that only humans can do"],answer:1,explain:"OpenAI's own launch material documents that GPT-5.6-Cyber produces shorter, less detailed outputs on open-ended vulnerability-report tasks. Cyber is tuned for narrow, offense-shaped requests. For long-form defensive review, Sol (via Blue or the public API) is the better tool. Route by task shape, not by 'strongest model wins.'"},{q:"Your org buys GPT-5.6-Cyber-powered services from a Daybreak partner (say, CrowdStrike). Can your engineers call GPT-5.6-Cyber directly from your own scripts?",options:["Yes, the partner passes through an API key to their customers","Yes, after signing the partner's DPA","No \u2014 the model access stays with the approved partner; customers get the output, not an API key","Only if you have hardware security keys enrolled for every seat"],answer:2,explain:"OpenAI's partner-customer split is deliberate: the KYC and per-engagement scope attestations sit with the partner, not the downstream customer. Buying partner services gives you the *output* of Cyber-powered work. Direct API access requires your own successful application to Daybreak Red (individual, org, or partner track)."},{q:"By what date do TOTP-only Daybreak accounts have to enrol hardware security keys?",options:["1 August 2026","10 August 2026","1 September 2026","31 December 2026"],answer:2,explain:"1 September 2026. TOTP is grandfathered only until that date. Every Daybreak account (Blue or Red) must present a WebAuthn / FIDO2 hardware key at sign-in after the deadline. If you're on TOTP today, procure and enrol before late August \u2014 key stock and enrolment take time."},{q:"What is OpenAI's Preparedness Framework rating for the GPT-5.6 family (Sol, Terra, and Cyber-on-Sol) on the Cybersecurity axis?",options:["Critical \u2014 the models can autonomously compromise hardened targets end-to-end","High \u2014 the models can find vulnerability components but were unable to carry out autonomous end-to-end attacks","Medium \u2014 the models refuse most offensive tasks","Low \u2014 the models have no meaningful cyber capability"],answer:1,explain:"High, not Critical. The GPT-5.6 system card is explicit that Sol and Terra can identify pieces of exploits but did not clear the Critical bar of autonomous end-to-end attacks on hardened systems. GPT-5.6-Cyber sits on the same base \u2014 a change in refusal policy, not a step change in capability. Marketing that describes 'autonomous offensive AI' is overselling what the vendor itself claims."}]}),"\n",(0,n.jsx)(t.h2,{id:"sources--further-reading",children:"Sources & further reading"}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.ul,{children:["\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.a,{href:"https://deploymentsafety.openai.com/gpt-5-6",children:"OpenAI Deployment Safety Hub \u2014 GPT-5.6 system card"}),' \u2014 the official preparedness classification (High, not Critical), the activation-classifier layer, and the explicit statement that Sol / Terra "were unable to carry out autonomous, end-to-end attacks."']}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.a,{href:"https://www.securityweek.com/openai-unveils-new-cybersecurity-model-gpt-5-6-cyber/",children:"SecurityWeek \u2014 OpenAI Unveils New Cybersecurity Model GPT-5.6-Cyber"})," \u2014 clearest single-page summary of the launch, tier structure, and named partner list."]}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.a,{href:"https://thehackernews.com/2026/08/openai-launches-gpt-56-cyber-with.html",children:"The Hacker News \u2014 OpenAI Launches GPT-5.6-Cyber with Reduced Safeguards for Exploit Development"}),' \u2014 dissects the 95% figure, calls out the "worse than Sol on open-ended reports" caveat, and cites the 26% / 53.9% AI-patch numbers.']}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.a,{href:"https://www.forbes.com/sites/jonmarkman/2026/08/11/openai-ships-gpt-56-cyber-its-first-offense-grade-hacking-model/",children:"Forbes \u2014 OpenAI Ships GPT-5.6-Cyber, Its First 'Offense-Grade' Hacking Model (Jon Markman, 2026-08-11)"}),' \u2014 the "offense-grade" framing, named CVE (CVE-2026-15903), and the hardware-key deadline.']}),"\n",(0,n.jsxs)(t.li,{children:[(0,n.jsx)(t.a,{href:"https://en.cryptonomist.ch/2026/08/11/openai-cybersecurity-program-gpt56-cyber/",children:"Cryptonomist \u2014 OpenAI cybersecurity program hits 95% zero-day detection with GPT-5.6-Cy